  upstream-apply-patch-instructions.md is a verbatim copy of lines 277-351 of

      codex-rs/core/prompt_with_apply_patch_instructions.md
        at commit a7edf37cb46b5fc4d50bd03df8e5999a86f602eb

  which is that file's whole `## apply_patch` section. It is reproduced without
  modification, typographic punctuation included, and a test pins its digest.

  index.mjs builds the model-facing description from that file by applying three
  substitutions to it, and no others. The list lives in `DESCRIPTION_ADAPTATIONS`
  and a test asserts that each applies exactly once and that the built text
  differs from the copy only where they say:

    1. The `## apply_patch` heading and the blank line after it are dropped.
    2. "Use the `apply_patch` shell command to edit files" reads "tool" instead
       of "shell command", because here it is a function tool taking one string
       argument.
    3. The trailing `You can invoke apply_patch like:` sentence and the
       `shell {"command":["apply_patch", ...]}` example are dropped, for the
       same reason.

  A previous revision of this notice named
  codex-rs/apply-patch/apply_patch_tool_instructions.md at commit
  88c7a4ff074df9e2161c947ca6d91bf824b9d6e6, and listed a fourth modification
  flattening non-ASCII punctuation. That file no longer exists at the ported
  commit, and the fourth modification was an artefact of retyping the text
  rather than copying it.

  This is not the description upstream registers. At the ported commit
  `create_apply_patch_freeform_tool` registers a 105-character description plus
  codex-rs/core/src/tools/handlers/apply_patch.lark as a grammar the decoder is
  constrained to; the harness this plugin mounts into registers JSON function
  tools and has no grammar seam, so the arm uses upstream's own text for a model
  reached without a grammar. Every operation the grammar defines is implemented,
  so nothing in the text describes something the tool refuses.

  parse-patch.mjs and codex-apply.mjs are ports of the functions below: the same
  algorithms in JavaScript, following their structure and their branch order
  closely enough that each ported function is recognisably the Rust one, though
  no line is a mechanical translation of a Rust line. index.mjs reproduces the
  order in which the function tool verifies and then applies an envelope, and
  the wording of the outcomes it reports.

      codex-rs/apply-patch/src/streaming_parser.rs   (`process_line`)
      codex-rs/apply-patch/src/parser.rs             (markers, `push_context_line`,
                                                      `check_patch_boundaries_lenient`)
      codex-rs/apply-patch/src/seek_sequence.rs      (`seek_sequence`)
      codex-rs/apply-patch/src/file_update.rs        (`derive_new_contents_from_chunks`,
                                                      `compute_replacements`,
                                                      `apply_replacements`)
      codex-rs/apply-patch/src/invocation.rs         (`try_verify_apply_patch_args`)
      codex-rs/apply-patch/src/lib.rs                (`print_summary`, apply order)
      codex-rs/core/src/tools/runtimes/apply_patch.rs (re-applying the raw patch)
        all at commit a7edf37cb46b5fc4d50bd03df8e5999a86f602eb

  Only the `NormalizeToLf` update mode is ported. It is the `#[default]` for
  `ApplyPatchFileUpdateMode`, and it is what a shipped Codex uses: the function
  tool selects `PreserveLineEndings` from `Feature::ApplyPatchPreserveLineEndings`
  (handlers/apply_patch.rs:60-70), which the feature registry declares
  `Stage::UnderDevelopment, default_enabled: false` (features/src/lib.rs:1005);
  the standalone binary selects it from an environment variable. That mode is
  the only reason upstream carries `context_line_indices`, so the ported chunks
  do not record it.

  parse-patch.mjs also transcribes six productions of

      codex-rs/core/src/tools/handlers/apply_patch.lark
        at commit a7edf37cb46b5fc4d50bd03df8e5999a86f602eb

  as a comment, for the reader; that is a transcription, not a copy of the file.

  __tests__/upstream/ is a verbatim copy of
  codex-rs/apply-patch/tests/fixtures/scenarios at commit
  a7edf37cb46b5fc4d50bd03df8e5999a86f602eb — 25 scenario directories, their
  README and their .gitattributes, unmodified. Upstream publishes them as a
  conformance suite "meant to be easily portable to other languages or
  platforms"; upstream-scenarios.test.mjs is that port. It is test material and
  the toolchain build excludes it, as it excludes every `__tests__` directory.

  __tests__/codex-fixtures.json is generated output, not source: codex-oracle.mjs runs the
  released `codex` binary over a temporary tree for each case and records what
  it printed and what the tree became. Where a case was refused, the recorded
  text is the binary's own diagnostic. Regenerating it requires a `codex` on
  PATH and is not part of the build or the test run.

  The recorded run used codex-cli 0.147.0, whose apply-patch sources sit at tag
  rust-v0.147.0 rather than at the commit ported above. The two differ in these
  files by a refactor — `UpdateFileChunk::default`, `push_context_line`, and the
  `context_line_indices` field — plus one behavioural change in
  `seek_sequence`'s end-of-file start position that applies to
  `PreserveLineEndings` only. Under `NormalizeToLf` they are the same
  algorithms, which is why the fixtures and the port agree on every case.

  An earlier version of this file said the description also dropped
  `*** Delete File:` and `*** Move to:` and their grammar productions. That was
  true when written and stopped being true when both operations were
  implemented. It is recorded here because a modification notice that
  under-reports what was reproduced is the kind that matters.

